{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_4536ippigituy5xhzq3s54puejasf3qvvtpfjzwxyegf5uduktca","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## Result: the translation is correct — no divergences found\n\nI made **no changes to `translation/src/lib.rs`**; the only source edit was adding `libloading = \"0.8\"` to `[dev-dependencies]`. `cargo check` was clean from the start.\n\n### Phase A — artifacts (crate root)\n| File | Content |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` | C `.so` exports exactly **1** symbol, `dataentry`. Rust `.so` exports it too → diff empty. The other 5 C functions are `static` (no dynamic symbol), and all 5 *are* translated — nothing was stubbed or skipped. |\n| `ERRORS.md` | **24 rows** grepped from every `return NULL` / `return -1` / `return 0` / range check / null check / constant in the C. |\n| `CONFIGS.md` | **30 rows** — the pruned cross-product of `mode` × count-selection × index/multiplier/addend shape × the full 4×3 lookup grid. |\n\n### Phases B–D — 53 differential tests, all via `libloading`\nBoth `.so`s are loaded through `dlopen`/`dlsym` and called only through the exported C symbol, so the `#[unsafe(no_mangle)]` wrapper and the ABI are under test. Every `CONFIGS.md` row and every `ERRORS.md` row has a passing test; error rows assert the **exact** sentinel (`-1`, `-2`, `0`), not \"both failed\".\n\n`run_verification.sh` runs the whole thing across **7 configurations** — all pass.\n\n### Three things I want to flag, because they were where the real risk was\n1. **`cargo test` does not emit the cdylib**, and a stale `target/release/*.so` would silently mask any fix. The harness therefore rebuilds *both* libraries from source on every run (Rust into a separate target dir to avoid the cargo lock).\n2. **The C relies on signed-overflow UB** (`100 + param2`, `total += value * multiplier`, `8 * param1`), which a compiler is free to exploit differently per optimization level. My first harness also configured the C with `-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release`, which is *not* the build the task documents. I fixed that to match exactly, then re-ran the full suite against the C rebuilt at `-O0/-O1/-O2/-O3/-Os`. The Rust's `wrapping_*` operations match gcc at every level.\n3. **A suite that never fails proves nothing.** `mutation_check.sh` injects 19 deliberate bugs (sentinels, base ids, default counts, loop bounds, table cells, string lengths, alloc size, switch dispatch) and requires each to be caught: **19/19 killed**. It also carries 3 negative controls that must *survive*, which pin down the dead C branches — `count <= 0`, `found->id == 0`, and `if (temp_value)`. That last one initially looked like a coverage blind spot; on inspection it is a **provably equivalent mutant** (when `temp_value == 0` the body computes `0 * multiplier == 0` and adds `0`), so I reclassified it as a control and added a genuinely observable mutation at the same site instead. Phase D also asserts the two `dlsym` results are different addresses, ruling out a vacuous self-comparison.\n\n`c_src/` is unmodified (md5s recorded); the C library is built out-of-tree into `translation/target/`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":575,"cache_read_input_tokens":105542,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":575,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed09d60b-f300-47ff-8aea-9d7dc1f16716","uuid":"0242e4fc-4c3a-4d88-bc43-daeb0c2670c5","timestamp":"2026-08-28T00:43:48.219Z"}
